# cmake-4.1.6-haiku-pie-is-pic.patch  (issue 715)
#
# Teach CMake that on Haiku, POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE for an *executable* means
# -fPIC, not -fPIE. This file is the explanatory note; the operative edit is the
# PATCH() function in recipes/cmake-4.1.6.recipe, which appends a block to
# Modules/Platform/Haiku.cmake inside the cmake source tree before BUILD().
#
# TOOLCHAIN INTEGRATION FIX, NOT A CUT. Nothing is removed from any build, no
# declared dependency changes, and the cmake that comes out is what Kitware
# intends everywhere except that it now asks this platform for the relocation
# model the platform actually links with.
#
# THE FAILURE
# -----------
# Any CMake target that is an executable with POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE on --
# CMake's default in many projects, and Ladybird's -- fails to link on arm64,
# one error per weak/COMDAT symbol:
#
#     FAILED: [code=1] bin/WebDriver
#     : && /boot/system/bin/clang++ ... -fPIE -pie -fuse-ld=lld ... -o bin/WebDriver
#     ld.lld: error: relocation R_AARCH64_ADR_PREL_PG_HI21 cannot be used against
#       symbol 'vtable for Core::Socket'; recompile with -fPIC
#     ld.lld: error: relocation R_AARCH64_ADD_ABS_LO12_NC cannot be used against
#       symbol 'AK::ErrorOr<void, AK::Error> AK::__format_value<AK::ByteString>(...)'
#       ; recompile with -fPIC
#     ld.lld: error: too many errors emitted, stopping now
#
# It is the first executable a C++ build reaches, and nothing about it is
# Ladybird-specific.
#
# WHY
# ---
# Haiku has no separate PIE link mode. Executables are ET_DYN, entered through
# start_dyn.o, so both system drivers link every executable as a shared object.
# This is not incidental, it is written into the specs -- in the buildtools
# checkout beside this repo:
#
#     gcc/config/aarch64/aarch64-haiku.h:68
#       #define LINK_SPEC "-m " TARGET_LINKER_EMULATION " %{!r:-shared} \
#         %{nostart:-e 0} %{shared:-e 0} %{!shared: %{!nostart: -no-undefined}} ...
#
#     gcc/config/haiku.h  (STARTFILE_SPEC)
#       "crti.o%s crtbeginS.o%s %{!shared:%{!nostart:start_dyn.o%s}} init_term_dyn.o%s"
#
# "%{!r:-shared}" is unconditional for every non-relocatable link: there is no
# spelling of the command line that links an executable without -shared. What
# distinguishes an executable is only that the *user* did not say -shared, so
# STARTFILE_SPEC adds start_dyn.o and LINK_SPEC leaves the entry point alone
# (a user -shared forces "-e 0"). Observed, on the native arm64 builder
# (clang 21 / gcc 13.3.0):
#
#     $ readelf -h /boot/system/bin/ls | grep Type
#       Type:                              DYN (Shared object file)
#
#     $ clang -### -fPIE -pie -fuse-ld=lld t.c -o t
#      "/boot/system/bin/ld.lld" "--eh-frame-hdr" "--enable-new-dtags" "-shared"
#        "--no-undefined" "-o" "t" .../crti.o .../crtbeginS.o .../start_dyn.o ...
#
#     $ gcc -### -fPIE -pie t.c -o tg
#      .../collect2 --eh-frame-hdr -m aarch64haiku -shared -no-undefined -EL -X
#        -pie -o tg .../crti.o .../crtbeginS.o .../start_dyn.o ...
#
# -shared appears in both, and clang drops -pie entirely. Producing a shared
# object, lld has to treat every default-visibility definition in the output as
# preemptible, and being in PIC mode it has neither a copy relocation nor a
# canonical PLT to fall back on, so a direct ADRP/ADD pair against one is a hard
# error. -fPIE told the compiler the opposite -- that this is a non-shared main
# program whose own definitions cannot be interposed -- which licenses exactly
# that pair:
#
#     $ clang -### -c -fPIE t.c    # -> "-mrelocation-model" "pic" "-pic-level" "2"
#                                  #    "-pic-is-pie"
#     $ clang -### -c -fPIC t.c    # -> ... "-pic-level" "2"   (no -pic-is-pie)
#     $ clang -### -c t.c          # -> ... "-pic-level" "2"   (no -pic-is-pie)
#
# so -fPIE is a *downgrade*: with no flag at all the driver already picks full
# PIC, the only model consistent with how it links. CMake's PIE substitution
# (Modules/Compiler/GNU.cmake:25, Modules/Compiler/Clang.cmake:29, both
# "-fPIE") turns that correct default into an incorrect one.
#
# THE SHARPER STATEMENT: -fPIE DOES NOT ADD PIE, IT SUPPRESSES HAIKU'S -fPIC
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The gcc side spells the mechanism out exactly. gcc/config/haiku.h:53,
# HAIKU_CC1_SPEC (pasted into CC1_SPEC):
#
#     %{fpic|fPIC|fpie|fPIE|fno-pic|fno-PIC|fno-pie|fno-PIE:;:-fPIC}
#
# Read: if the user named any of those eight flags, emit nothing; otherwise
# emit -fPIC. Haiku does not default to "whatever the target default is" -- it
# *actively injects -fPIC*, and opts out the instant the user expresses any
# opinion about the relocation model. So POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE -> -fPIE is
# not a request layered on top of a PIC default; it is a request that *disables*
# the platform's own -fPIC and installs a weaker model in its place. That is why
# the linker's advice -- "recompile with -fPIC" -- is not a generic suggestion
# but literally the correct instruction: it is asking for the flag the platform
# would have supplied had CMake said nothing at all.
#
# PRECISELY WHICH SYMBOLS, AND A CORRECTION TO THE ISSUE'S WORDING
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Issue 715 describes these as "cross-DSO COMDAT symbols". That framing does not
# survive testing and matters for anyone reproducing it. A reference to a symbol
# only *defined* in a shared library is fine -- it still goes through the GOT --
# and a two-file cross-DSO case links clean under -fPIE:
#
#     lib.cpp:  Sock::~Sock() {}  int Sock::fd() { return 7; }
#               int useLib(Sock& s) { return s.fd(); }
#     main.cpp: int main() { Sock s; return useLib(s) + s.fd(); }
#     $ clang++ -fPIC -shared -o libsock.so lib.cpp
#     $ clang++ -fPIE -pie -fuse-ld=lld -o x main.cpp -L. -lsock   # exit 0
#
# What actually fails is a weak/COMDAT default-visibility definition emitted
# into *the executable's own object*, whose address the generated code forms
# directly. The lld diagnostic says so: ">>> defined in .../main.o". The issue's
# own nm evidence agrees -- "vtable for Core::Socket" is listed as `V` in
# Session.cpp.o, i.e. present in the object being linked. A class defined in the
# program, or a template instantiated there, is the everyday case; a C++ ctor
# loads the vtable address directly, so it shows up immediately. Ladybird links
# with -Xlinker -Bsymbolic-non-weak-functions, which already binds non-weak
# functions locally, which is why only the weak symbols surface there.
#
# WHAT WAS REJECTED
# -----------------
# * Passing -pie through to lld as well, so that the output is a real PIE and
#   -fPIE becomes self-consistent (which is what happens with gcc, whose ld sees
#   "-shared ... -pie" and honours the later flag -- `g++ -fPIE -pie` links this
#   case fine). lld refuses outright:
#       ld.lld: error: -shared and -pie may not be used together
#   Making clang's Haiku driver emit -pie instead of -shared would also change
#   the ELF shape of every clang-linked executable on the platform, which is the
#   loader's and the ELF segment model's business, not a CMake integration fix.
#   Two concrete things in this tree would break if anyone tried it, both read
#   from the DeBeOS sources (runtime_loader is another issue's territory; this is
#   cited as a reason *not* to go that way, nothing here touches it):
#
#     - src/system/runtime_loader/arch/arm64/arch_relocate.cpp:214-216 ends its
#       switch with "default: TRACE(...); return B_BAD_DATA;". It handles
#       R_AARCH64_NONE, ABS64, GLOB_DAT, JUMP_SLOT, RELATIVE, IRELATIVE,
#       TLS_DTPMOD64, TLS_DTPREL64 and TLSDESC -- and nothing else. R_AARCH64_COPY
#       does not appear anywhere in runtime_loader, nor do ABS32/ABS16 or any
#       PREL form. A non-PIC main program is precisely what emits copy
#       relocations, so making executables genuinely non-PIC would produce images
#       the loader rejects outright.
#
#     - src/system/runtime_loader/runtime_loader.cpp:498-500 is
#       "if (elfHeader->e_entry == 0) { /* we don't like to open shared
#       libraries */ status = B_NOT_AN_EXECUTABLE; }". Because executables and
#       libraries are both ET_DYN here, a non-zero e_entry is the *only* thing
#       that distinguishes them -- and LINK_SPEC above forces "-e 0" whenever the
#       user passes -shared. The executable/library distinction on this platform
#       is therefore load-bearing on the exact spec behaviour that -fPIE's
#       premise contradicts.
#
# * Patching the clang and gcc drivers to make -fPIE an alias for -fPIC on this
#   target. It would fix every build system at once, but it silently redefines a
#   documented flag, has to be done twice in two separate toolchain packages, and
#   is strictly more than is needed: no build system on this platform has a
#   legitimate reason to ask for -fPIE, and CMake is the one that does.
#
# * Turning POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE off in the consumer project. That is the
#   workaround, not the fix, and AGENTS.md rules it out as a deliverable. It
#   happens to work only because the driver's no-flag default is already PIC --
#   which is the same observation this patch acts on, except this patch has CMake
#   *ask* for PIC rather than rely on the absence of a flag.
#
# WHERE IT HAS TO GO, AND WHY NOT A Platform/Haiku-<id>-<lang> FILE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Modules/Compiler/<id>.cmake sets the generic -fPIE, and it is included from
# CMake<lang>Information.cmake *before* any platform file. The only later hook
# for Haiku today is that same file's second inclusion:
#
#     # CMakeCXXInformation.cmake
#     include(Compiler/${CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID}-CXX OPTIONAL)              # -fPIE set here
#     ...
#     include(Platform/...-${CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID}-CXX OPTIONAL RESULT_VARIABLE _INCLUDED_FILE)
#     if (NOT _INCLUDED_FILE)
#       include(Platform/${CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME} OPTIONAL)                    # <-- Haiku.cmake, again
#     endif ()
#
# so the override belongs in Platform/Haiku.cmake. Adding a
# Platform/Haiku-Clang-CXX.cmake or Platform/Haiku-GNU-CXX.cmake to carry it
# instead -- the structurally tidier option, and how CYGWIN-GNU is organised --
# would set _INCLUDED_FILE and thereby suppress that second inclusion. Haiku.cmake
# *needs* it: its secondary-architecture detection shells out to
# `${CMAKE_C_COMPILER} -print-search-dirs`, which cannot work at the first,
# pre-compiler inclusion from CMakeSystemSpecificInformation.cmake. Moving the
# PIE override into a platform-compiler file would therefore silently break x86
# secondary-architecture include and library search. That is the reason for the
# otherwise odd-looking foreach over language names.
#
# THE FIX
# -------
# Appended to Modules/Platform/Haiku.cmake:
#
#     foreach(lang C CXX ASM ASM-ATT OBJC OBJCXX Fortran)
#       set(CMAKE_${lang}_COMPILE_OPTIONS_PIE "-fPIC")
#       set(CMAKE_${lang}_LINK_OPTIONS_PIE "")
#       set(CMAKE_${lang}_LINK_OPTIONS_NO_PIE "")
#     endforeach()
#
# COMPILE_OPTIONS_PIC keeps its "-fPIC" from Compiler/GNU.cmake, so PIC and PIE
# now agree, as they must on a platform with one relocation model. Both link
# option sets are emptied: nothing extra is needed at link time, and -no-pie
# cannot be honoured at all -- PIE is not something this platform can be asked
# for or refused.
#
# _CMAKE_<lang>_PIE_MAY_BE_SUPPORTED_BY_LINKER is deliberately left YES.
# check_pie_supported() then try-compiles with the (empty) PIE link options and
# reports success, which is the honest answer here: every executable is position
# independent. Forcing it NO, as CYGWIN-GNU does, would make projects that call
# check_pie_supported() and require PIE fail for no reason.
#
# VERIFIED (native arm64 builder i-01886408618b237de, clang 21, lld, cmake 4.1.6)
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Minimal reproduction -- a weak COMDAT vtable defined in the executable:
#
#     $ clang++ -fPIC -shared -o libsock.so lib.cpp
#     $ nm -C main_pie.o | grep MySock
#     0000000000000000 V vtable for MySock
#     $ clang++ -fPIE -pie -fuse-ld=lld -o m_pie main.cpp -L. -lsock
#     ld.lld: error: relocation R_AARCH64_ADR_PREL_PG_HI21 cannot be used against
#       symbol 'vtable for MySock'; recompile with -fPIC
#     >>> defined in /tmp/main-39163a.o
#     >>> referenced by main.cpp
#     >>>               /tmp/main-39163a.o:(MySock::MySock())
#     ... exit 1, no output file
#     $ clang++ -fPIC -fuse-ld=lld -o m_pic main.cpp -L. -lsock
#     ... exit 0, m_pic produced
#
# The same sources through a real CMake project (CMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE
# ON, add_library(sock SHARED) + add_executable(app) + target_link_libraries),
# built twice against a copy of the shipped CMAKE_ROOT with and without the
# appended block:
#
#     BEFORE   app CXX_FLAGS = -O3 -DNDEBUG -fPIE
#              build exit 2, "relocation R_AARCH64_ADR_PREL_PG_HI21 cannot be
#              used against symbol 'vtable for MySock'; recompile with -fPIC",
#              no app produced
#     AFTER    app CXX_FLAGS = -O3 -DNDEBUG -fPIC
#              sock CXX_FLAGS = -O3 -DNDEBUG -fPIC
#              link.txt carries no -pie
#              build exit 0, app + libsock.so produced, app is ET_DYN
#
# check_pie_supported() under the fix: PIE_SUPPORTED=1 NO_PIE_SUPPORTED=1, no
# warning. CMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE=OFF still configures and builds, and
# emits no -no-pie.
#
# AND IT RUNS. The same project reconfigured with the link flags a real lld
# build on this platform uses -- "-fuse-ld=lld -Wl,-z,max-page-size=4096", which
# is already what graviton/ladybird/patches/ladybird-haiku.patch passes -- builds
# and executes:
#
#     app CXX_FLAGS = -O3 -DNDEBUG -fPIC
#     app link.txt  = clang++ ... -fuse-ld=lld -Wl,-z,max-page-size=4096 ... -o app
#     build exit 0, app (136184 bytes) + libsock.so produced, 4 PT_LOADs
#     $ ./app ; echo $?
#     9                      # main returns 9: it ran, and ran correctly
#
# and the matching control, same link flags but the *stock* Modules/Platform/
# Haiku.cmake restored, still fails exactly as before:
#
#     app CXX_FLAGS = -O3 -DNDEBUG -fPIE
#     build exit 2
#     ld.lld: error: relocation R_AARCH64_ADR_PREL_PG_HI21 cannot be used
#       against symbol 'vtable for MySock'; recompile with -fPIC
#     ld.lld: error: relocation R_AARCH64_ADD_ABS_LO12_NC cannot be used
#       against symbol 'vtable for MySock'; recompile with -fPIC
#     no app produced
#
# So the patch is the only variable: with it the project links and runs, without
# it the identical command line does not link.
#
# An earlier attempt to run the output did fail with "runtime_loader: .../app:
# Could not map image: Bad data", and that was *not* issue 720 biting -- it was
# this note's author omitting max-page-size=4096 from the link. Recorded here
# because the wrong conclusion is an easy one to reach: issue 720 is about the
# loader's inter-segment hole budget, and with 4096-byte max-page-size the holes
# collapse to zero, so a correctly linked image maps. If an lld-linked
# executable ever fails to map *with* that flag, that is a new finding and not
# this patch and not issue 720.
#
# UPSTREAM: Modules/Platform/Haiku.cmake is CMake's own file and this gap is
# CMake's, not DeBeOS's -- the same block would be correct in cmake.git, and
# every Haiku architecture is affected (aarch64 is merely where lld makes it
# fatal rather than silently wrong). Offering it there is a separate, optional
# path and is not assumed; per project policy nothing here is submitted upstream.
# The in-repo home is this recipe.
#
# Retire when: a shipped cmake carries PIE/PIC handling for Haiku of its own, at
# which point the PATCH() guard already makes this a no-op and the function can
# be deleted.
